<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lewis Pelly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lewis Pelly was a British officer and diplomat who served as Political Resident in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> from 1862 to 1872. After the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and the Qataris in 1867, he signed an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-thani">Muhammad bin Thani</a> in 1868 that effectively recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> as distinct from Bahrain. He later served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and as a Member of Parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lewis-pelly.jpg" alt="Lewis Pelly" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Lewis_Pelly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lewis Pelly was the son of John Hinde Pelly of Hyde House, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, and a nephew of Sir John Henry Pelly, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>. Educated at Rugby School, he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s service in 1840 and was commissioned in the Bombay Army in 1841. He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, took part in James Outram's corruption enquiry at Baroda in 1851–2, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-war">Anglo-Persian War</a> of 1856–7 was aide-de-camp to General John Jacob, about whom he published a book in 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After serving as secretary and then chargé d'affaires of the British legation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, he went on a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1860 and rode on, in uniform, most of the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, impressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Sir Bartle Frere</a>, who became his patron. As political agent and consul at Zanzibar he sided with Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majid-bin-said">Majid bin Said</a> in the dispute over the subsidy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. As Political Resident in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> he surveyed for the Persian Gulf telegraph cable, visited Faisal bin Turki, ruler of the Second Saudi State, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1865, and in 1868 sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> with four warships, after which Muhammad bin Khalifa Al Khalifa was deposed in favour of his brother Ali.</p><p>In 1872 and 1873 he accompanied Frere on the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Appointed agent to the governor general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> in 1873, he replaced Robert Phayre as resident at Baroda in November 1874 and in January 1875 had its ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-gaekwad">Malhar Rao Gaekwad</a>, arrested and tried by a commission; the result was inconclusive, but Malhar Rao was deposed. In 1877 he met an envoy of the Afghan emir Sher Ali Khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, but the talks led nowhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Returning to the United Kingdom in 1878, Pelly married Amy Henrietta Lowder. In 1885 he was elected Conservative member for the new constituency of North Hackney. As a director of the Imperial British East Africa Company, his votes on a grant for a railway survey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> were disallowed in 1892 on J. G. Swift MacNeill's motion, a precedent that persisted in parliamentary practice. He died at Falmouth on 22 April 1892 and was buried at Merstham in Surrey. His English translation of 37 Persian ta'ziyeh dramas appeared in 1879 as The Miracle Play of Hasan and Husain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lewis Pelly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a destructive war between them, the British envoy <b>Lewis Pelly</b> signed an agreement with Muhammad bin Thani of Doha in 1868, recognising him as the peninsula's leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Sheikhdom of Qatar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Minchinhampton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Falmouth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.71,-2.19&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Minchinhampton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lewis Pelly"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4884 5705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4884 5705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.71,-2.19&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
